## Why This Is Asked
This asks for deep bit-manipulation skill: a palindrome across 128 bits is a nontrivial symmetry that can be checked in constant time with a reversal network, not with loops. It reveals understanding of endian-ness and 128-bit layout.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit word as {uint64 hi, lo}
- Bit-reversal networks using masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F..., etc.
- Palindrome property across all 128 bits
- Edge-case tests (all zeros, ends-set pattern)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
static inline uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.lo = reverse64(x.hi); r.hi = reverse64(x.lo); return r;
}
int isPalindromicBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r = reverse128(x);
  return (x.hi == r.hi) && (x.lo == r.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support streaming 128-bit palindromicity with partial data?
- What are the performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to compose 128-bit reversal from 64-bit primitives without branching.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit bit reversal algorithm
- Cross-block mapping for 128-bit reversal
- Constant-time, no-branch implementation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo_rev = rev64(x.lo);
  uint64_t hi_rev = rev64(x.hi);
  return (uint128){ .hi = lo_rev, .lo = hi_rev };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement rev64 efficiently if not provided by compiler?
- How does this scale if you had a 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests fundamental bitwise thinking and the ability to apply a classic parity reduction without branches. It mirrors low-level optimizations used in data integrity checks.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-folding for parity
- Right-shifts for subtraction-free folding
- Constant-time bit-twiddling

## Code Example

```javascript
// (Note: actual signature shown in question; C-like syntax used for clarity)
uint8_t parity32(uint32_t x) {
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return (uint8_t)((x & 0xF) & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs? 
- What are potential pitfalls with compiler optimizations on such patterns?

